  quotes: [
    { text: "Political advertising has always been “parent–child”: the politician telling you what is good for you. Now the “child” can speak.", page: "PDF 325" },
    { text: "It is a tool: it serves multiple masters. The medium is not the agenda.", page: "PDF 326" }
  ],
  idea: "Chapter 13 is a tour of four places the Digital Revolution has changed: how we choose governments, how governments serve us, where we travel, and how we tackle social issues (plus a Hall of Fame case on Nescafé). Young's framing line: the classic recipes in *Ogilvy on Advertising* for politics, tourism and good causes mostly still hold. What digital changes is how far and how personally you can push them.\n\n**David's rule, broken once.** Ogilvy had a rule of no political advertising. Ogilvy & Mather has broken it rarely, only through an associated agency and only when staff were personally committed. The big exception was India's 2014 election: Piyush Pandey, Ogilvy's creative chief for India and South Asia, did Narendra Modi's campaign through a subsidiary shop.\n\n**Parent–child becomes a conversation.** Young's key observation (first quote): political ads used to be a parent lecturing a child. Digital lets the child answer back, and pass things on. Two decisions made the Modi campaign work:\n1. Persuading the BJP party to centre the campaign on **one individual**, not the party.\n2. Writing the “Modi moment” in the **vernacular**, the words voters actually used, not the stiff formal language of national TV.\n\nThe result was a handful of **simple phrases anyone could repeat**. They spread through social media to people who had it, then by word of mouth to people who didn't. Modi was a genuine early adopter (Pandey says Modi ran the digital side himself; a caption notes he had 20 million more Twitter followers than Donald Trump). Yet Young's verdict is that digital was *the icing on a very traditional media cake*.\n\n**Can social be the cake?** That was the hope of the Arab Spring. One protester summed up the roles: Facebook to schedule the protests, Twitter to coordinate, YouTube to tell the world. Young says social media galvanised action early on, but later it fragmented the majority as factions fought each other. The anonymous “We Are All Khaled Said” Facebook page was credited with sparking Egypt's 2011 revolution; today Facebook hosts views for and against it. Hence the second quote: the platform carries anyone's message.\n\n**A stunt built to be shared.** In 2012 Ogilvy's Tunis office fought voter apathy with an event made for social media: a giant poster of the deposed dictator Ben Ali reappeared, and when angry people tore it down, a warning underneath said dictatorship can return and told them to vote.\n\n> The lesson for ZDub: the platform gives you reach and a two-way channel. The message (in the customer's own words, simple enough to repeat) and the plan behind it still do the work.",
