  source: [{ doc: "Ogilvy on Advertising in the Digital Age (Miles Young, 2017)", section: "Chapter 13 · Digital Transformations · Digital Politics (Obama 2008/2012, US election 2016) · PDF pp. 327–333", who: "Miles Young" }],
  quotes: [
    { text: "But the hard work – the important work – was traditional database management: merging the donor database with the field-activist database with the voter database.", page: "PDF 330" },
    { text: "At that point you suddenly begin to look at people in multi-dimensions, whereas before they were handled incredibly transactionally.", page: "PDF 330 (Thomas Gensemer)" }
  ],
  idea: "The standard story of Barack Obama's two campaigns is that new media came of age and pushed traditional media aside. Young calls that a remarkable misunderstanding. The real story is more complex, and more useful.\n\n**Where it started.** In 2004, Howard Dean's run for the Democratic nomination caught the tail end of the anti-war protests and the hangover of the dot-com bust. Thomas Gensemer, Joe Rospars and others (the founders of what became Blue State Digital) used **email marketing and database management** to hold together an anti-war coalition of young and old. Money poured in, which changed how everyone saw the economics of campaigning.\n\n**Obama 2008.** They told campaign manager David Plouffe their role had to sit at the centre of everything: fundraising, marketing and, above all, the field organisation. He agreed. They started early, had an inspiring candidate and used Facebook and Twitter energy. But the important work was the unglamorous part (first quote): merging donor, activist and voter databases so you could see each person in several dimensions (second quote).\n\n**What that bought.** $500 million raised online in 2008 and $690 million in 2012, spent largely on **traditional TV** for a huge share-of-voice advantage. More importantly, millions of people got invested. A field manager in Cuyahoga, Ohio, could see that voter X had given a few hundred dollars and knocked on doors, so he was worth building a whole experience around to get more of his labour. The money funded the biggest field operation ever, with one playbook. The captions add classic direct-marketing technique: member-get-member, contests, A/B testing. The famous “I will be outspent” email went through **17 tested versions** and raised **$2.4 million in one day**. A supporter in Ohio asked the website for the official colours and logo and painted his whole barn. (Young notes the US Voter File and long campaign cycles make the full recipe hard to export.)\n\n**2016, read the same way.** Clinton raised over $1 billion to Trump's $650 million. Trump's tweets and rallies earned a huge free-media advantage, but his digital director Brad Parscale ran a traditional, data-driven, constantly tested campaign, closer to Obama's model, with real fundraising impact. It also included targeted attempts to suppress turnout among key Clinton supporters. Clinton's team focused on stylish Facebook videos for young voters, dropped traditional advertising at the end, and discussed but never ran TV in swing states like Pennsylvania and Wisconsin.\n\n> The pattern: glamour channels get the headlines; merged data, relentless testing, mobilised supporters and plain old reach win. It's David Ogilvy's direct-marketing faith, scaled up.",
  keyPoints: [
    "The myth: social media replaced traditional media. The reality: database work, field organisation and TV",
    "Merge your lists so you see each person in several dimensions (donor + volunteer + voter), not one transaction",
    "Test relentlessly: “I will be outspent” went through 17 versions and raised $2.4 million in a day",
    "Classic direct-marketing tools still drive it: member-get-member, contests, A/B tests",
    "2016: the better-funded side lost to the side with tighter data, testing and targeting; stylish videos for the young weren't enough",
    "Targeting can be turned to voter suppression, a use Young records and Zach should never copy"
  ],
