  idea: "David Ogilvy gave travel a whole chapter of *Ogilvy on Advertising*; he called himself “the Grand Panjandrum of travel advertising”. International travel grew in his lifetime from 25 million trips in 1950 to 435 million in 1990, and has since passed 1 billion a year. Digital now helps with researching, buying and celebrating a trip. Young's key point is the first quote: communication's job is to **move the experience earlier**, so people can almost test the place before they pay.\n\n**Flip the perception.** “Incredible !ndia” faced hostile perceptions (sweaty, dirty, nasty) and turned them around with stunning photography: different, yes, but stimulating the senses rather than unsettling them. The campaign gave India a distinctive identity (the “!” standing in for the I), showed familiar sights in unfamiliar ways (the Taj Mahal reflected upside down), showed the country's scenic range and linked visitors to India's welcome. It always mixed traditional and digital: buzz, offers, a digital home for each state's programme. Piyush Pandey, who told his mother he liked “to make people travel”, advertised two of the states. One was Madhya Pradesh, huge but never a first choice: Ogilvy used *ombromanie*, the old art of hand shadows, to evoke its sights without photography, backed by a rustic jingle sung by local actor Raghubir Yadav.\n\n**Pre-experience through your friends** (second quote). Everyone knows the envy and vicarious joy of a friend's Facebook holiday feed. Cape Town had no money for TV, so it hijacked the place people already share holidays: **send your Facebook profile on a five-day trip**. Your timeline filled with personalised content of what you could see and do (it took 150 POV videos, 10,000 photos and 400 status updates to build), friends saw it too, and you could win the real trip. Cape Town gained awareness and an implicit peer recommendation, and bookings rose 118 per cent the following year.\n\n**Let the citizens sell it.** A country is the sum of its people, so Sweden, its tourism hit by recession, set up a number anyone in the world could call to reach a random Swede (5,000 signed up at first; 36,000 became ambassadors). It drew 190,000 calls from 186 countries, 13,000 in the first week. Callers learned Swedes are open, direct, proud of their country beyond the cities, and keen for visitors.\n\n**Make the job the ad.** Tourism Queensland advertised “the best job in the world”: island caretaker on a UNESCO site, $100,000 salary, duties including swimming and snorkelling. Over 1.4 million people from 200 countries applied with videos online.\n\n> For a business that sells an experience people are nervous about, the job is the same: let them feel it, through friends and locals, before they commit.",
