  tools: [
    {
      id: "memory-drill",
      type: "flashcards",
      title: "Memory drill",
      cards: [
        { tag: "Principle", front: "What changed about political advertising in the digital age (Young's “parent–child” point)?", back: "It used to be parent–child: the politician telling you what's good for you. Now the “child” can speak back and pass it on. Modi's campaign used the voters' vernacular and simple, repeatable phrases.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 325" },
        { tag: "Principle", front: "“The medium is not the ___.” What did Young learn from the Arab Spring?", back: "…agenda. Social media galvanised action early, then fragmented the majority. It's a tool that serves multiple masters.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 326" },
        { tag: "Campaign", front: "Obama's “I will be outspent” email: how many tested versions, and what did it raise?", back: "17 versions tested before the broad send; $2.4 million in one day.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 328" },
        { tag: "Principle", front: "What was the “hard work” behind Obama's digital campaigns?", back: "Traditional database management: merging donor, field-activist and voter databases so people were seen in multiple dimensions, not transactionally. Money raised online was spent largely on TV.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 330" },
        { tag: "Stat", front: "Healthcare.gov launch: visits vs completed transactions?", back: "20 million visits, only 500,000 completed transactions. Causes: perfectionism, rigid procurement, divided leadership, resistance to bad news.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 333–338" },
        { tag: "Term", front: "GDS's mantra for GOV.UK?", back: "“The product is the service is the marketing.” Strip out everything between the citizen and the task. Mocked as “boring.com”; won a D&AD Black Pencil.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 337" },
        { tag: "Principle", front: "What's the job of digital communication in tourism?", back: "To bring the actual experience forward in the buying cycle: let people pre-experience the destination, as close to testing it as possible.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 340–341" },
        { tag: "Campaign", front: "Send Your Facebook Profile to Cape Town: how did it work and what happened?", back: "Your profile took a 5-day virtual trip (150 POV videos, 10,000 photos, 400 statuses) that your friends saw, with a chance to win it for real. Bookings rose 118% the next year.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 341–342" },
        { tag: "Campaign", front: "The Swedish Number (Random Swede): the mechanic and the results?", back: "Call a number and talk to a random Swede. 190,000 calls from 186 countries; 36,000 Swedes signed up as ambassadors.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 341–343" },
        { tag: "Principle", front: "Young's three steps for any social task?", back: "Raise the status and importance of the issue → identify your supporters → mobilize them into action. Mobilization is what digital adds.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 346" },
        { tag: "Principle", front: "The three rules of digital social responsibility?", back: "1) Avoid anything that smells of green-washing. 2) Be prepared to shock. 3) Have a clear ask: what to do and how it improves the issue.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 347" },
        { tag: "Campaign", front: "WWF Thailand “Chor Chang”: what did people do?", back: "Removed the Thai letter for “elephant” from their names. 1.3 million joined; the PM backed a crackdown and Thailand changed its ivory laws.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 346, 351" },
        { tag: "Campaign", front: "Nescafé's reinvention: the icons, the platform line and the fan growth?", back: "A red accent on the wordmark and a modern red mug; “It all starts with a Nescafé”; from 2 million sleepy followers to 40 million engaged fans.", ref: "Ch 13 · PDF 354–356" },
        { tag: "Term", front: "Greenberg's business model split at R/GA?", back: "60% agency, 30% consulting, 10% incubator. He also believes in a nine-year cycle of reinvention.", ref: "Ch 14 · PDF 362" },
        { tag: "Principle", front: "Why did Nike drop the FuelBand hardware, according to Young?", back: "Focus, like an athlete: hardware is a means to an end; it's the Nike+ community (40 million) that counts.", ref: "Ch 14 · PDF 364–365" },
        { tag: "Term", front: "“Ichi o kiite juu o shiru”: meaning, and whose principle?", back: "“When someone says one, you jump to ten”: imply, don't spell it out. Kagami's ideal; Uniqlock embodies it.", ref: "Ch 14 · PDF 370–371" },
        { tag: "Ogilvy said", front: "What did David Ogilvy say after Martin Nisenholtz explained his work, and what should Martin have said?", back: "“This is complete bullshit.” Martin should have said he'd made word of mouth measurable, in plain language instead of tech jargon.", ref: "Ch 14 · PDF 378" },
        { tag: "Campaign", front: "Spiff's Swedish Post campaign in one line?", back: "Influencers got a letter to go online, saw a banner greeting them by name offering lobster, clicked once, and had fresh lobster at the door 30 minutes later.", ref: "Ch 14 · PDF 381–382" },
        { tag: "Principle", front: "Chuck Porter's “Don't feed the gorilla”?", back: "Challengers shouldn't act like the category leader. MINI skipped the standard outdoor launch and toured Minis on top of SUVs in 22 cities.", ref: "Ch 14 · PDF 391" },
        { tag: "Principle", front: "CP+B's definition of advertising, and Young's warning about it?", back: "“Anything that makes our clients famous.” But fame without brand can be a dangerous thing.", ref: "Ch 14 · PDF 391, 394" }
      ]
    }
  ]
